Before changing code, inspect the complete current flow:

- Email Report button/icon
- "Preparing report" state
- Screenshot generation
- DOM element being captured
- Screenshot library/tool being used
- ".eml" generation
- MIME structure
- Inline image / Content-ID implementation
- Release dashboard React components
- Relevant CSS/Tailwind styles
- Any responsive breakpoints
- Any "transform", "scale", fixed heights, absolute positioning, flex/grid constraints
- Any "html2canvas", Playwright, browser screenshot, or equivalent implementation

Identify the actual root cause of the overlap rather than adding zoom-specific CSS hacks.

Do not immediately modify code before understanding the current implementation.

---

Step 2 — Create a deterministic email-report rendering environment

The email report screenshot should use a dedicated rendering environment.

Use a fixed report width such as approximately:

1200px–1440px

Choose the value that best fits the existing application and email layout.

The root report container should have deterministic dimensions, for example:

width: 1200px;
min-width: 1200px;
max-width: 1200px;
box-sizing: border-box;

Do not allow the report layout to depend on:

window.innerWidth
window.devicePixelRatio
current browser zoom
current dashboard responsive breakpoint

unless technically required by the screenshot implementation.

---

Step 3 — Prevent layout-related text overlap

Inspect the release dashboard CSS carefully.

Look specifically for:

- Fixed "height" on containers containing dynamic text
- "overflow: hidden"
- "position: absolute"
- "transform"
- "scale"
- "translate"
- Flexbox containers with constrained dimensions
- Grid columns with incorrect sizing
- "white-space: nowrap"
- "text-overflow"
- Incorrect "line-height"
- Percentage-based heights
- "vh"-based sizing
- Responsive breakpoint-dependent dimensions
- Nested elements with conflicting width/height constraints

Dynamic text should be allowed to wrap naturally.

Prefer:

height: auto;
min-height: ...;
overflow-wrap: anywhere;
word-break: break-word;
white-space: normal;

where appropriate.

Do NOT blindly remove existing styles. Determine which styles are actually responsible for the overlap.

